{
  "title": "Final Walkthrough Checklist: What to Check Before Closing",
  "metaDescription": "Use this customizable, printable final walkthrough checklist before closing to verify repairs, test systems, and protect your contract rights.",
  "h1": "Final Walkthrough Checklist",
  "introText": "During a final walkthrough, you confirm that your purchase home matches the condition required by your sales contract before you sign your closing papers. You verify that all agreed repairs are complete with proper receipts, all fixtures and personal property included in the purchase agreement remain in place, no new structural or cosmetic damage has occurred, and every major mechanical system and home appliance operates as expected. If you find any discrepancies, incomplete repairs, or unexpected property damage, you must raise those issues with your real estate agent immediately so you can resolve them before completing closing.\n\nThe interactive tool on this page builds a customized checklist for your specific purchase. Select whether you are purchasing an existing resale home, a condo, or new construction, and note whether the seller agreed to repairs or has already vacated the property. You can also select specific features such as private wells, septic systems, or swimming pools. The tool drops irrelevant checks automatically based on your selections and updates your active item count. When you are ready to visit the property, use the Print or save as PDF button to generate a clean worksheet complete with name, date, and property address lines.",
  "toolHeading": "Your Final Walkthrough Checklist",
  "toolSubheading": "Select your property details to customize your printable checklist.",
  "howItWorks": "The final walkthrough serves to verify that the home meets the specific conditions stated in your purchase agreement. It is not a second comprehensive home inspection or a chance to negotiate new cosmetic requests. By contrast, your final walkthrough checks that the property remains in the promised condition, that normal occupancy has not caused new damage, and that all contract commitments are fulfilled before ownership transfers. You can coordinate this step alongside the milestones in our [home buying checklist](/checklists/home-buying-checklist/).\n\nIf your purchase agreement required the seller to make specific repairs, review that documentation prior to entering the property. You should bring copies of all signed repair amendments, paid contractor invoices, and formal receipts. As you walk the property, locate each completed repair and confirm the work was finished in a professional manner. Verify that the contractor specified in your contract performed the work and that all agreed scopes of work match the written invoices.\n\nTake your time walking through every room to test fixtures, surfaces, and functional components. Open and close every door and window, test each latch and deadbolt, check window screens for tears, and inspect walls and ceilings for fresh water stains or impact damage from moving furniture. Test electrical outlets, light switches, ceiling fans, and installed light fixtures. Turn on the heating and air conditioning systems to confirm they produce warm and cool air through the registers. In bathrooms and kitchens, turn on every faucet to verify water pressure, check for leaks under sinks, flush every toilet, and run appliances like the dishwasher, garbage disposal, and stove. According to [NFPA guidance on smoke alarms](https://www.nfpa.org/education-and-research/home-fire-safety/smoke-alarms), alarms should be installed inside each bedroom, outside every separate sleeping area, and on every level of the home. Verify that units are present and test them during your review.\n\nNew construction homes require a distinct evaluation process compared to resale properties. Instead of checking a previous owner's move-out condition, you will compare the home against the builder's final punch list. Inspect the home to confirm that every punch-list defect has been corrected. Confirm that the local municipality has issued an official certificate of occupancy. Collect copies of all builder warranties. On the exterior, inspect the final grading around the foundation to confirm that soil slopes away from the structure, and locate the electric, gas, and water meters to confirm they are properly connected.\n\nHandover items represent the final operational details of the property transfer. Verify that the seller has left all garage door openers, house keys, mailbox keys, gate fobs, and security alarm codes. Gather instruction manuals and warranty documents for built-in appliances and mechanical equipment. If the property uses a private water supply, the [EPA private well guidelines](https://www.epa.gov/privatewells) note that well owners are responsible for their own water safety and should test regularly for bacteria and nitrates. For properties with private waste systems, the [EPA septic guidelines](https://www.epa.gov/septic) recommend regular professional inspections and routine tank pumping. If you discover incomplete repairs, missing appliances, or new damage, notify your real estate agent immediately before signing closing papers. Your agent can help you pursue appropriate contract remedies, which may include requiring the seller to complete repairs, issuing a closing credit, holding funds in escrow until completion, or delaying closing until the home satisfies the contract. You can review how financial adjustments affect your settlement using our [closing cost calculator](/mortgage/closing-cost-calculator/).\n\nBring a copy of your contract, the seller repair receipts, and your printed checklist to the property. Test every system methodically, check each room thoroughly, and address any contract violations before you sign your final closing documents. Your sales contract and real estate agent determine the exact scheduling of the walkthrough.",
  "workedExample": "In our internal comparison, we ran the checklist rules for two example homes to evaluate how the list adapts to different transaction types. The complete checklist library contains 30 items. In the first scenario, we evaluated a typical resale purchase where the seller agreed to repair requests and had already vacated the premises. The rules preserved 21 items from the 30 item library, keeping all agreed repair receipt checks, seller cleanout requirements, and general room inspections while dropping new-construction items.\n\nIn the second scenario, we evaluated a newly constructed home with no negotiated repair addenda. For this property, the rules retained 22 items from the 30 item library. The tool dropped the previous seller cleanout checks and repair invoices, replacing them with specific new-construction requirements such as the builder punch list, municipal certificate of occupancy, builder warranty, final foundation grading, and utility meter verifications.",
  "commonMistakes": [
    "Treating the walkthrough as a complete technical inspection instead of verifying contract compliance and agreed repairs.",
    "Failing to bring copies of the signed repair addendum, contractor invoices, and payment receipts to verify completed repairs.",
    "Assuming repairs were completed properly without physically examining the specific work areas.",
    "Rushing through the property without testing basic plumbing fixtures, electrical switches, and major kitchen appliances.",
    "Signing the closing paperwork before securing a formal written resolution for discovered damages or missing fixtures."
  ],
  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "What do you check during a final walk through?",
      "answer": "You check that the property matches the physical condition required by your purchase contract. You verify that all agreed repairs are finished with supporting receipts, no new property damage exists, included fixtures remain, and all plumbing, electrical, and heating systems operate properly."
    },
    {
      "question": "Can a buyer walk away at final walkthrough?",
      "answer": "Whether you can cancel a contract depends entirely on the terms and contingencies of your signed purchase agreement. If you uncover substantial unresolved damage or contract violations, speak with your real estate agent or a real estate attorney. They can explain your contractual rights and legal remedies before you proceed to settlement."
    },
    {
      "question": "When does the final walkthrough happen?",
      "answer": "The final walkthrough typically takes place shortly before your formal closing. The specific day and time are governed by the provisions in your purchase agreement and coordinated between the real estate agents."
    },
    {
      "question": "What if the seller didn't finish the repairs?",
      "answer": "If agreed repairs are incomplete or lack contractor receipts, alert your real estate agent before closing. Your agent can negotiate potential solutions with the seller, such as a monetary credit at settlement, escrow holdbacks for the repair funds, or a postponed closing date until the seller completes the work."
    },
    {
      "question": "Is a final walk through necessary?",
      "answer": "A final walkthrough is your last opportunity to confirm property condition before taking legal ownership. If you skip this inspection and sign closing papers, you absorb financial responsibility for any unaddressed damage, missing property, or non-functioning equipment."
    },
    {
      "question": "What should I check in a new construction walkthrough?",
      "answer": "In a new construction walkthrough, compare the home against the builder punch list to verify that all minor defects were resolved. Confirm that the local jurisdiction issued the certificate of occupancy, collect builder warranty paperwork, check final ground grading, and ensure all utility meters are active."
    },
    {
      "question": "What should the seller leave behind?",
      "answer": "The seller must leave behind all fixtures and personal property specified in your purchase agreement. They should also leave all house keys, mailbox keys, garage door remotes, security access codes, and equipment instruction manuals."
    },
    {
      "question": "Can I print this final walkthrough checklist?",
      "answer": "Yes. Once you select your property details in the tool, click the Print or save as PDF button. The tool formats your customized checklist onto a clean page with designated lines for your name, inspection date, and property address."
    }
  ],
  "slug": "final-walkthrough-checklist"
